<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vandal conquest of North Africa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/429">429</a>–<a href="/years/439">439 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vandal conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> was the seizure of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans between 429 and 439 CE. Crossing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> under King Gaiseric, they captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 439 and established a kingdom that controlled much of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> place this disruption of Roman North Africa in the context of the Garamantes' decline.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Vandal conquest of North Africa</b> between 429 and 439 CE disrupted Mediterranean trade demand at the same time, so that both supports of the economy weakened together.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians since Edward Gibbon have called the <b>Vandal conquest of North Africa</b> the greatest single blow to the Western Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
